WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.380
Congress passed the Jones Act to guarantee a wartime ready American merchant fleet, and ended

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.140
up with a handful of aging ships and cargo moving by truck instead.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:12.920
This is Unintended Consequences, Episode 90.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:19.120
After World War I, American policymakers watched foreign flag ships carry the bulk of U.S.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:19.640
exports.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:24.780
They worried the nation would again be caught short of tonnage if another war broke out.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:30.680
Senator Wesley L. Jones of Washington, chair of the Senate Commerce Committee, introduced

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.880
the Merchant Marine Act of 1920.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:38.440
The law closed the coast-wise trade to any vessel that was not built in the United States,

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:44.420
owned by U.S. citizens, registered under the U.S. flag, and crewed by American mariners.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:50.240
The measure enjoyed broad support from shipyard unions, naval officers, and both parties.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:55.760
It passed with little debate and was signed by President Wilson on 5 June 1920.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:01.320
At the time the logic appeared straightforward, a protected domestic market would keep

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:06.660
American yards busy, maintain a pool of trained seafarers, and ensure that the government

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.960
could requisition tonnage without negotiating with foreign owners.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:16.240
Lawmakers had just lived through the scramble of 1917, when the United States had to charter

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:21.560
foreign vessels and hastily convert passenger liners because domestic bottoms proved insufficient

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.660
for the scale of troop movements to Europe.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:29.840
The Jones Act took effect immediately for trade between the contiguous states, Alaska, Hawaii,

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:30.840
and Puerto Rico.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.940
In its first decade, the protected trades did see new construction.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:40.380
Bethlehem and Newport Newsyards delivered several large passenger cargo liners for the

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:41.960
inter-coastal and Hawaii runs.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:47.900
Proponents pointed to the steady employment of roughly 50,000 American seafarers in the early

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:53.660
1920s. Skeptics in the shipping press noted that the new vessels were already more expensive to

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:59.020
operate than foreign-flad competitors. Those differentials were accepted as the necessary price

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:05.200
of self-sufficiency. Over the following decades, the protected fleet did not expand. It contracted.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:12.160
Between 1945 and 2020, the number of Jones Act eligible cargo vessels fell from more than

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:20.500
1,200 to fewer than 100. Most of them were built in the 1970s or 1980s, and now approach or exceed

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:26.540
the end of their economic lives. A new US-built container ship can cost three to four times as much

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:32.640
as an equivalent vessel built in South Korea or China. Ship operators responded by shifting cargo

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:38.820
to trucks and rail, wherever geography allowed. Goods moving between the east and west coasts now

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:44.660
travel overland rather than through the Panama Canal on American bottoms. For island economies,

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:50.720
the effect was sharper. A 2019 government accountability office review found that the cost of shipping

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.160
a 40-foot container from Jacksonville to San Juan was roughly double the cost of shipping the

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:01.800
same container from Jacksonville to the Dominican Republic. Hawaii and Alaska reported similar

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:08.020
markups on fuel, building materials, and food. Puerto Rican manufacturers lost ground to competitors

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:14.160
in the Dominican Republic and Mexico, who could use cheaper foreign flag feeders. With fewer ships,

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:20.000
the maritime academies graduate officers who often find work on foreign flag vessels or leave the

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:26.020
industry. The US military has increasingly turned to foreign flag vessels under the maritime security

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:33.060
program to meet surge requirements. Reform efforts have surfaced periodically. In 2013 and again

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:39.260
in 2018, members of Congress introduced bills to allow limited foreign built vessels in the Puerto Rico

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:45.320
trade. Both measures died in committee after opposition from maritime unions and Gulf Coast shipyards.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:51.380
Temporary waivers were granted after Hurricane Maria and Irma, yet the waivers were narrow and

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:58.700
short-lived. As of 2024, the maritime administration lists only 92 privately-owned Jones Act cargo

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:05.560
ships, most over 30 years old. No comprehensive redesign of the statute has occurred. Shielding

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:10.600
an industry from all external competition tends to raise its costs faster than its productivity.

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:16.720
The protected capacity can shrink even while the legal barrier remains in place. When the

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:22.260
protected activity is also the one the policy claims to value most, the contradiction becomes

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:28.960
self-reinforcing. Policy makers facing similar choices today might ask whether the instrument they

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:34.700
are reaching for will still produce the capability they need once every competitor has been removed from

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:40.220
the market. The Jones Act's history suggests that the answer depends less on the strength of the

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:46.300
initial intention than on whether the chosen mechanism can adapt when the arithmetic of costs and demand

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:52.220
changes. That's the case for today. The lessons tend to travel further when you share them,

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:57.060
so send this one to someone who'd find it useful, and subscribe wherever you're listening.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:02.840
